('111.47.c.6.J',4,'In a medical test for a disease, a false positive result corresponds to which error?','["no error", "sampling error only", "Type II error", "Type I error"]'::jsonb,3,'The test says the disease is present when it is not.','original'),
('111.47.c.7.A',3,'A scatterplot shows points rising from left to right in a tight line. How would you describe the association?','["strong positive linear", "strong negative linear", "no association", "weak negative"]'::jsonb,0,'Both variables increase together.','original'),
('111.47.c.7.A',3,'What does an outlier in a scatterplot look like?','["a point on the line", "a point far from the overall pattern", "a cluster", "the mean point"]'::jsonb,1,'It does not fit the trend.','original'),
('111.47.c.7.A',4,'An influential point in a scatterplot is one that does what?','["lies exactly on the line", "is the mean point", "greatly changes the regression line when removed", "has no effect"]'::jsonb,2,'Its removal shifts the fit.','original'),
('111.47.c.7.A',3,'Points on a scatterplot curve upward like a bowl. Is a linear model appropriate?','["yes, if r is positive", "yes, for outliers", "yes, always", "no, the pattern is not linear"]'::jsonb,3,'A curved pattern needs a different model.','original'),
('111.47.c.7.B',3,'A line of best fit has slope 2.5 and y-intercept 10. Which equation is it?','["ŷ = 10 + 2.5x", "ŷ = 10x", "ŷ = 2.5 + 10x", "ŷ = 2.5x − 10"]'::jsonb,0,'ŷ = intercept + slope · x.','original'),
('111.47.c.7.B',3,'For ŷ = 3 + 2x, what is the predicted y when x = 5?','["15", "13", "8", "10"]'::jsonb,1,'3 + 2(5).','original'),
('111.47.c.7.B',4,'In a model for height (cm) predicted from age, the slope is 6. What does that mean?','["age increases 6 years per cm", "there is no relationship", "height is predicted to increase 6 cm per year of age", "height is 6 cm at age 0"]'::jsonb,2,'The slope is change in y per unit x.','original'),
('111.47.c.7.C',3,'Which measure tells how well a linear model explains variation in y?','["the sample size", "the mean of y", "the range", "r², the coefficient of determination"]'::jsonb,3,'r² is the proportion of variation explained.','original'),
('111.47.c.7.C',4,'Two linear models have r² = 0.45 and r² = 0.82. Which fits better?','["the model with r² = 0.82", "they fit equally", "the model with r² = 0.45", "neither can be compared"]'::jsonb,0,'Higher r² means more variation explained.','original'),
('111.47.c.7.C',4,'A residual plot with a clear curved pattern suggests what?','["the residuals are zero", "a linear model is not appropriate", "the linear model is perfect", "the data are random"]'::jsonb,1,'A good linear fit has residuals scattered randomly.','original'),
('111.47.c.7.D',4,'Compared with least squares, how is the median-median line affected by outliers?','["it ignores all data", "it is more affected", "it is less affected", "it is equally affected"]'::jsonb,2,'Medians resist extreme values.','original'),
('111.47.c.7.D',4,'A least-squares line minimizes the sum of what?','["the absolute values of x", "the data values", "the products of residuals", "the squared residuals"]'::jsonb,3,'That is the least-squares criterion.','original'),
('111.47.c.7.D',4,'A line fit by minimizing the sum of absolute residuals is generally less sensitive to what?','["outliers", "the sample size", "the slope", "the units"]'::jsonb,0,'Absolute values do not square large residuals.','original'),
('111.47.c.7.E',4,'A point far to the right of the other data in a scatterplot, away from the line, is likely to do what to the regression line?','["remove the intercept", "pull the line toward it", "make the slope zero", "leave the line unchanged"]'::jsonb,1,'High-leverage points have strong influence.','original'),
('111.47.c.7.E',4,'Removing an influential point from a data set leads to what?','["r² staying the same", "the same line of best fit", "a noticeably different line of best fit", "no residuals"]'::jsonb,2,'That is why the point is influential.','original'),
('111.47.c.7.E',3,'Using dynamic graphing technology, you drag one point and the line moves a lot. What does this show?','["the data are exact", "the line is perfect", "the correlation is zero", "that point is influential"]'::jsonb,3,'The fit depends strongly on it.','original'),
('111.47.c.7.F',4,'A model predicts a car''s value from age. The y-intercept is $25,000. What does this represent?','["the predicted value of a car at age 0", "the car''s color", "the value after 25 years", "the yearly loss"]'::jsonb,0,'The intercept is the predicted y at x = 0.','original'),
('111.47.c.7.F',4,'A model predicts weight from height. Predicting a weight for a height far outside the data range is called what?','["residual analysis", "extrapolation", "interpolation", "sampling"]'::jsonb,1,'Predictions outside the data range are risky.','original'),
('111.47.c.7.F',3,'The correlation coefficient r = −0.9. What does this indicate?','["no relationship", "a weak positive relationship", "a strong negative linear relationship", "a strong positive relationship"]'::jsonb,2,'The sign shows the direction and the size shows the strength.','original'),
('111.47.c.7.F',3,'A residual is defined as what?','["the slope times x", "the mean of y", "the predicted y minus the observed y", "the observed y minus the predicted y"]'::jsonb,3,'residual = y − ŷ.','original')
